<h2>Architecture Notes</h2>

<p>All components are built with React + TypeScript using <code>@webflow/react</code>'s <code>declareComponent()</code> and the <code>@webflow/data-types</code> property system. Each component lives in a pair of files:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>ComponentName.tsx</code> — the React implementation</li>
  <li><code>ComponentName.webflow.tsx</code> — the Webflow integration wrapper that declares properties for the Designer's property panel</li>
</ul>

<p>At runtime each component renders as a <code>&lt;code-island&gt;</code> custom element with Declarative Shadow DOM. <code>props.Slot()</code> emits a native <code>&lt;slot&gt;</code> placeholder; user-authored slot contents live in light DOM and are projected at runtime. This has implications worth knowing about — see the <a href="https://developers.webflow.com/code-components">Webflow Code Components docs</a> for the full model.</p>

<h2>Webflow Designer Preview Caveat</h2>

<p>Interactive code components (Dropdown, Countdown Timer, 3D Card, VCard, etc.) do <strong>not</strong> run their client-side JavaScript in Webflow's Designer Preview mode. Webflow sets <code>data-hydrate="false"</code> on the <code>&lt;code-island&gt;</code> host in Preview, which means <code>useEffect</code> hooks never run (or are torn down). Static rendering still works correctly, but interactivity is inert.</p>

<p>Always verify interactive behavior on the Published site, not in Designer Preview.</p>

